}
```

The client can now do the thing your current design makes impossible: keep the 499 successes and resubmit only the failures after fixing them. Include each item's index (or echo back a client-supplied id) so results can be matched to submissions without positional guessing, and give each rejection the same error shape your single-record endpoint uses - clients should learn one error vocabulary, not two.

On status codes: a batch that was understood and processed returns 200 (or 207 Multi-Status, if your stack supports it honestly) with the per-item truth inside. Reserve the bare 400 for when the batch *itself* is unintelligible - malformed JSON, 501 items when the limit is 500. The nervousness to examine is whether your items are truly independent: if record 200 spends the budget that record 201 needed, they're one operation, not a batch, and all-or-nothing is the truthful design. Batch semantics are for independent items.

